      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-18 (P0.1: the pre-dial handshake delivers the PAIR, not half of it).\n\nTHE BREAK. Cortex mints a per-pairing signing secret (R-8) and signs every DelegatedTask with it,\nand /api/v1/bridge/token already OFFERED it back to the owner. The daemon parsed only `token` and\nthrew the secret away, so its step-1 signature check ran against an empty string and EVERY real\ndelegated task denied. `org` was worse: BridgeTokenResponse had no org field at all, nothing ever\nwrote BridgeConfig.org, and the verifier's step 3 refuses `t.org !== ctx.org` as cross-org\naddressing. Two gaps on one path, and the CHECK ORDER hid the second behind the first - the canary\nonly ever reported 'bad signature'.\n\nTHE FIX. `org` joins `signingSecret` on the mint response, gated identically (owner of a live,\nnon-revoked pairing; both omitted otherwise, so an unknown or revoked pairing still fails closed).\nThe daemon learns both on every dial and persists them, and its verify context reads them through a\nMUTABLE binding, so a rotation arriving on a re-dial takes effect without restarting the process.\n\nWHY IT SURVIVED WEEKS UNSEEN. The bridge repo had no CI, and the one suite that would have caught\nthis constructed its daemon with the org already set and the platform jwtSecret as the signing key:\ngreen against a harness that never reproduced the handshake. The canary now takes the pairing's REAL\nsecret from the registry, and a full-serve e2e drives serve() so the daemon LEARNS both values from\nthe mint response before a file delegation is asserted 'ok'. It tests the handshake instead of\nassuming it.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-18 (P0.1: the pre-dial handshake delivers the PAIR, not half of it).\n\nTHE BREAK. Cortex mints a per-pairing signing secret (R-8) and signs every DelegatedTask with it,\nand /api/v1/bridge/token already OFFERED it back to the owner. The daemon parsed only `token` and\nthrew the secret away, so its step-1 signature check ran against an empty string and EVERY real\ndelegated task denied. `org` was worse: BridgeTokenResponse had no org field at all, nothing ever\nwrote BridgeConfig.org, and the verifier's step 3 refuses `t.org !== ctx.org` as cross-org\naddressing. Two gaps on one path, and the CHECK ORDER hid the second behind the first - the canary\nonly ever reported 'bad signature'.\n\nTHE FIX. `org` joins `signingSecret` on the mint response, gated identically (owner of a live,\nnon-revoked pairing; both omitted otherwise, so an unknown or revoked pairing still fails closed).\nThe daemon learns both on every dial and persists them, and its verify context reads them through a\nMUTABLE binding, so a rotation arriving on a re-dial takes effect without restarting the process.\n\nWHY IT SURVIVED WEEKS UNSEEN. The bridge repo had no CI, and the one suite that would have caught\nthis constructed its daemon with the org already set and the platform jwtSecret as the signing key:\ngreen against a harness that never reproduced the handshake. The canary now takes the pairing's REAL\nsecret from the registry, and a full-serve e2e drives serve() so the daemon LEARNS both values from\nthe mint response before a file delegation is asserted 'ok'. It tests the handshake instead of\nassuming it.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-18 (P0.1: the pre-dial handshake delivers the PAIR, not half of it).\n\nTHE BREAK. Cortex mints a per-pairing signing secret (R-8) and signs every DelegatedTask with it,\nand /api/v1/bridge/token already OFFERED it back to the owner. The daemon parsed only `token` and\nthrew the secret away, so its step-1 signature check ran against an empty string and EVERY real\ndelegated task denied. `org` was worse: BridgeTokenResponse had no org field at all, nothing ever\nwrote BridgeConfig.org, and the verifier's step 3 refuses `t.org !== ctx.org` as cross-org\naddressing. Two gaps on one path, and the CHECK ORDER hid the second behind the first - the canary\nonly ever reported 'bad signature'.\n\nTHE FIX. `org` joins `signingSecret` on the mint response, gated identically (owner of a live,\nnon-revoked pairing; both omitted otherwise, so an unknown or revoked pairing still fails closed).\nThe daemon learns both on every dial and persists them, and its verify context reads them through a\nMUTABLE binding, so a rotation arriving on a re-dial takes effect without restarting the process.\n\nWHY IT SURVIVED WEEKS UNSEEN. The bridge repo had no CI, and the one suite that would have caught\nthis constructed its daemon with the org already set and the platform jwtSecret as the signing key:\ngreen against a harness that never reproduced the handshake. The canary now takes the pairing's REAL\nsecret from the registry, and a full-serve e2e drives serve() so the daemon LEARNS both values from\nthe mint response before a file delegation is asserted 'ok'. It tests the handshake instead of\nassuming it."
    },
