      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-18 (P1: the daemon EXECUTES steps, and the I9 secret lifecycle that makes that safe).\n\nWHAT CHANGED ON THE WIRE. `tool.invoke` used to be answered with a named refusal and `secret.deliver`\nwas DROPPED without being stored. Both were gated on this slice: running a step on someone's machine\non remote instruction needed the gates, the credential lifecycle and an evidence channel first. The\nframe pair now carries real work, and `tool.result` gains ONE new optional field, `screenshotB64`\n(Rule 7: additive - no field removed, no type narrowed).\n\nTWO GATES, BOTH FAIL CLOSED.\n  1. ADVERTISEMENT. A browser step needs `desktop.automation`, a bash step needs `local.bash`. Both\n     are OFF by default; `resolveCapabilities` adds them only from an explicit config opt-in made by\n     the human at the machine. Cortex then intersects the advertisement with the org's capability\n     grant (I-3, default deny), so advertising authorises nothing on its own.\n  2. TIER-2 ENABLEMENT (ADR-002), checked before EVERY step, bash and browser alike. Today it is\n     DERIVED from gate 1 - defence-in-depth and one flip point for a future runtime toggle, not an\n     independent second factor. Recorded as a limitation in docs/decisions.md rather than sold as a\n     design win.\n\nTHE CORTEX SEAM, FIXED. `server.ts` mapped a browser step to `local.filesystem` - the FILE-delegation\nrail, which is neither what the daemon advertises for automation nor what an executor gates on. An\norg that had correctly granted `desktop.automation` saw every browser step refused; one that had\ngranted `local.filesystem` for file reads had silently also authorised browser automation. The map\nand its dispatch now live in `bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts`, where they can be imported and asserted.\n\nTHE I9 LIFECYCLE (P1.3, co-requisite - it lands WITH the executor, never after). On `secret.deliver`\nthe daemon holds the env map keyed by invocationId with values as BUFFERS: a JS string cannot be\nzeroized, a Buffer can. RAM only - never config.json, never the ledger, never a log, not even the\nenv-var NAMES. The matching bash invoke injects at spawn and zeroizes in a `finally`; an unmatched\ndelivery is swept and zeroized on TTL.\n\nTHE EGRESS REDACTOR wraps `send` ONCE, inside DaemonRuntime, so EVERY outbound frame passes through\nit - child stdout/stderr, browser observation text, denial reasons, provider bodies. It is the mirror\nof Cortex's ingress filter one hop earlier, which is the only version that also keeps a value off the\nnetwork and out of a proxy log. Its lifetime deliberately OUTLIVES the hold: the hold is zeroized in\n`withChildEnv`'s finally, which runs BEFORE the tool.result carrying that child's stdout is built -\ntying the two together left exactly the frame most likely to carry the echo unfiltered. Found by\ntest/security/outbound-redaction.test.ts failing on that window.\n\nSTRUCTURAL IDS AND THE SCREENSHOT ARE NOT REDACTED, deliberately: substituting inside a join key\ncorrupts the join, and a screenshot is an image. `session.push.storageState` is left byte-exact -\nmangling a captured session mints a valid, correctly-encrypted, USELESS Cofre item.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-18 (P1: the daemon EXECUTES steps, and the I9 secret lifecycle that makes that safe).\n\nWHAT CHANGED ON THE WIRE. `tool.invoke` used to be answered with a named refusal and `secret.deliver`\nwas DROPPED without being stored. Both were gated on this slice: running a step on someone's machine\non remote instruction needed the gates, the credential lifecycle and an evidence channel first. The\nframe pair now carries real work, and `tool.result` gains ONE new optional field, `screenshotB64`\n(Rule 7: additive - no field removed, no type narrowed).\n\nTWO GATES, BOTH FAIL CLOSED.\n  1. ADVERTISEMENT. A browser step needs `desktop.automation`, a bash step needs `local.bash`. Both\n     are OFF by default; `resolveCapabilities` adds them only from an explicit config opt-in made by\n     the human at the machine. Cortex then intersects the advertisement with the org's capability\n     grant (I-3, default deny), so advertising authorises nothing on its own.\n  2. TIER-2 ENABLEMENT (ADR-002), checked before EVERY step, bash and browser alike. Today it is\n     DERIVED from gate 1 - defence-in-depth and one flip point for a future runtime toggle, not an\n     independent second factor. Recorded as a limitation in docs/decisions.md rather than sold as a\n     design win.\n\nTHE CORTEX SEAM, FIXED. `server.ts` mapped a browser step to `local.filesystem` - the FILE-delegation\nrail, which is neither what the daemon advertises for automation nor what an executor gates on. An\norg that had correctly granted `desktop.automation` saw every browser step refused; one that had\ngranted `local.filesystem` for file reads had silently also authorised browser automation. The map\nand its dispatch now live in `bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts`, where they can be imported and asserted.\n\nTHE I9 LIFECYCLE (P1.3, co-requisite - it lands WITH the executor, never after). On `secret.deliver`\nthe daemon holds the env map keyed by invocationId with values as BUFFERS: a JS string cannot be\nzeroized, a Buffer can. RAM only - never config.json, never the ledger, never a log, not even the\nenv-var NAMES. The matching bash invoke injects at spawn and zeroizes in a `finally`; an unmatched\ndelivery is swept and zeroized on TTL.\n\nTHE EGRESS REDACTOR wraps `send` ONCE, inside DaemonRuntime, so EVERY outbound frame passes through\nit - child stdout/stderr, browser observation text, denial reasons, provider bodies. It is the mirror\nof Cortex's ingress filter one hop earlier, which is the only version that also keeps a value off the\nnetwork and out of a proxy log. Its lifetime deliberately OUTLIVES the hold: the hold is zeroized in\n`withChildEnv`'s finally, which runs BEFORE the tool.result carrying that child's stdout is built -\ntying the two together left exactly the frame most likely to carry the echo unfiltered. Found by\ntest/security/outbound-redaction.test.ts failing on that window.\n\nSTRUCTURAL IDS AND THE SCREENSHOT ARE NOT REDACTED, deliberately: substituting inside a join key\ncorrupts the join, and a screenshot is an image. `session.push.storageState` is left byte-exact -\nmangling a captured session mints a valid, correctly-encrypted, USELESS Cofre item.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-18 (P1: the daemon EXECUTES steps, and the I9 secret lifecycle that makes that safe).\n\nWHAT CHANGED ON THE WIRE. `tool.invoke` used to be answered with a named refusal and `secret.deliver`\nwas DROPPED without being stored. Both were gated on this slice: running a step on someone's machine\non remote instruction needed the gates, the credential lifecycle and an evidence channel first. The\nframe pair now carries real work, and `tool.result` gains ONE new optional field, `screenshotB64`\n(Rule 7: additive - no field removed, no type narrowed).\n\nTWO GATES, BOTH FAIL CLOSED.\n  1. ADVERTISEMENT. A browser step needs `desktop.automation`, a bash step needs `local.bash`. Both\n     are OFF by default; `resolveCapabilities` adds them only from an explicit config opt-in made by\n     the human at the machine. Cortex then intersects the advertisement with the org's capability\n     grant (I-3, default deny), so advertising authorises nothing on its own.\n  2. TIER-2 ENABLEMENT (ADR-002), checked before EVERY step, bash and browser alike. Today it is\n     DERIVED from gate 1 - defence-in-depth and one flip point for a future runtime toggle, not an\n     independent second factor. Recorded as a limitation in docs/decisions.md rather than sold as a\n     design win.\n\nTHE CORTEX SEAM, FIXED. `server.ts` mapped a browser step to `local.filesystem` - the FILE-delegation\nrail, which is neither what the daemon advertises for automation nor what an executor gates on. An\norg that had correctly granted `desktop.automation` saw every browser step refused; one that had\ngranted `local.filesystem` for file reads had silently also authorised browser automation. The map\nand its dispatch now live in `bridge/daemon-step-seam.ts`, where they can be imported and asserted.\n\nTHE I9 LIFECYCLE (P1.3, co-requisite - it lands WITH the executor, never after). On `secret.deliver`\nthe daemon holds the env map keyed by invocationId with values as BUFFERS: a JS string cannot be\nzeroized, a Buffer can. RAM only - never config.json, never the ledger, never a log, not even the\nenv-var NAMES. The matching bash invoke injects at spawn and zeroizes in a `finally`; an unmatched\ndelivery is swept and zeroized on TTL.\n\nTHE EGRESS REDACTOR wraps `send` ONCE, inside DaemonRuntime, so EVERY outbound frame passes through\nit - child stdout/stderr, browser observation text, denial reasons, provider bodies. It is the mirror\nof Cortex's ingress filter one hop earlier, which is the only version that also keeps a value off the\nnetwork and out of a proxy log. Its lifetime deliberately OUTLIVES the hold: the hold is zeroized in\n`withChildEnv`'s finally, which runs BEFORE the tool.result carrying that child's stdout is built -\ntying the two together left exactly the frame most likely to carry the echo unfiltered. Found by\ntest/security/outbound-redaction.test.ts failing on that window.\n\nSTRUCTURAL IDS AND THE SCREENSHOT ARE NOT REDACTED, deliberately: substituting inside a join key\ncorrupts the join, and a screenshot is an image. `session.push.storageState` is left byte-exact -\nmangling a captured session mints a valid, correctly-encrypted, USELESS Cofre item."
