      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P1 follow-up: the CORTEX half of the credential seam).\n\nThe P1 annotation to the left describes the daemon's obligations. These three are Cortex's, and all\nthree were wrong on the hosted side while the daemon's half was correct.\n\nTHE ORDER OF THE SEAM. `runStep` ran authorise -> deliver -> invoke, and the ONLY per-machine\nauthorisation (`isCapabilityGranted`, I-3, default deny) lived INSIDE invoke - so it ran LAST. Every\n`envRefs`-bearing step therefore redeemed the single-use nonce, unwrapped a Cofre item, armed the\ningress filter, put the PLAINTEXT on the machine's socket and wrote a Registo \"use\" row, and was\nrefused only afterwards; the daemon then held that value in RAM for the delivery TTL. Reproduced\nbefore the fix against a real item and a real socket. The grant is now the FIRST statement of\n`runStep`, re-read per step so a mid-run revoke stops the next credential, and the refusal carries\n`retryable: false` - unflagged, `local-command.ts` marked it recoverable and the engine re-delivered\non every retry. The check inside `invokeTool` STAYS: it guards every other caller of the coordinator.\n\nTHE DELIVERY TTL HAD NO TRIGGER. The pending map holds no credential (the nonce is redeemed before\nanything is unwrapped) but it does hold a live permission to unwrap one onto a socket, and `sweep()`\nwas reachable only from `authoriseDelivery` - so an orphan expired only if another delivery happened\nto follow it, and `deliverSecrets` never compared `createdAt` at all. Now: sweep on the redeem path\n(expiry by comparison, not by a timer having fired), one UNREF'd timeout armed for the oldest entry,\nand a drop from the socket `close` handler beside `releasePairingSecrets`.\n\nTHE INGRESS FILTER SKIPPED THE OBSERVATION. `tool.result.output` was scrubbed only when it was a\nSTRING, and every payload P1 made reachable is an object - `{stdout, stderr, exitCode}` and\n`{url, title, heading, accessibilitySnapshot}`. So the likeliest echo of all, a bash step's stdout,\nwas the one field the hosted filter did not look at, while the annotation to the left called this\nfilter the mirror of the daemon's egress redactor. It now walks `output` depth-bounded. BOTH\nredactors stay: the daemon's keeps the value off the network, this one still applies to a daemon\nthat is older, misconfigured or compromised. `screenshotB64` remains untouched - it is an image.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P1 follow-up: the CORTEX half of the credential seam).\n\nThe P1 annotation to the left describes the daemon's obligations. These three are Cortex's, and all\nthree were wrong on the hosted side while the daemon's half was correct.\n\nTHE ORDER OF THE SEAM. `runStep` ran authorise -> deliver -> invoke, and the ONLY per-machine\nauthorisation (`isCapabilityGranted`, I-3, default deny) lived INSIDE invoke - so it ran LAST. Every\n`envRefs`-bearing step therefore redeemed the single-use nonce, unwrapped a Cofre item, armed the\ningress filter, put the PLAINTEXT on the machine's socket and wrote a Registo \"use\" row, and was\nrefused only afterwards; the daemon then held that value in RAM for the delivery TTL. Reproduced\nbefore the fix against a real item and a real socket. The grant is now the FIRST statement of\n`runStep`, re-read per step so a mid-run revoke stops the next credential, and the refusal carries\n`retryable: false` - unflagged, `local-command.ts` marked it recoverable and the engine re-delivered\non every retry. The check inside `invokeTool` STAYS: it guards every other caller of the coordinator.\n\nTHE DELIVERY TTL HAD NO TRIGGER. The pending map holds no credential (the nonce is redeemed before\nanything is unwrapped) but it does hold a live permission to unwrap one onto a socket, and `sweep()`\nwas reachable only from `authoriseDelivery` - so an orphan expired only if another delivery happened\nto follow it, and `deliverSecrets` never compared `createdAt` at all. Now: sweep on the redeem path\n(expiry by comparison, not by a timer having fired), one UNREF'd timeout armed for the oldest entry,\nand a drop from the socket `close` handler beside `releasePairingSecrets`.\n\nTHE INGRESS FILTER SKIPPED THE OBSERVATION. `tool.result.output` was scrubbed only when it was a\nSTRING, and every payload P1 made reachable is an object - `{stdout, stderr, exitCode}` and\n`{url, title, heading, accessibilitySnapshot}`. So the likeliest echo of all, a bash step's stdout,\nwas the one field the hosted filter did not look at, while the annotation to the left called this\nfilter the mirror of the daemon's egress redactor. It now walks `output` depth-bounded. BOTH\nredactors stay: the daemon's keeps the value off the network, this one still applies to a daemon\nthat is older, misconfigured or compromised. `screenshotB64` remains untouched - it is an image.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P1 follow-up: the CORTEX half of the credential seam).\n\nThe P1 annotation to the left describes the daemon's obligations. These three are Cortex's, and all\nthree were wrong on the hosted side while the daemon's half was correct.\n\nTHE ORDER OF THE SEAM. `runStep` ran authorise -> deliver -> invoke, and the ONLY per-machine\nauthorisation (`isCapabilityGranted`, I-3, default deny) lived INSIDE invoke - so it ran LAST. Every\n`envRefs`-bearing step therefore redeemed the single-use nonce, unwrapped a Cofre item, armed the\ningress filter, put the PLAINTEXT on the machine's socket and wrote a Registo \"use\" row, and was\nrefused only afterwards; the daemon then held that value in RAM for the delivery TTL. Reproduced\nbefore the fix against a real item and a real socket. The grant is now the FIRST statement of\n`runStep`, re-read per step so a mid-run revoke stops the next credential, and the refusal carries\n`retryable: false` - unflagged, `local-command.ts` marked it recoverable and the engine re-delivered\non every retry. The check inside `invokeTool` STAYS: it guards every other caller of the coordinator.\n\nTHE DELIVERY TTL HAD NO TRIGGER. The pending map holds no credential (the nonce is redeemed before\nanything is unwrapped) but it does hold a live permission to unwrap one onto a socket, and `sweep()`\nwas reachable only from `authoriseDelivery` - so an orphan expired only if another delivery happened\nto follow it, and `deliverSecrets` never compared `createdAt` at all. Now: sweep on the redeem path\n(expiry by comparison, not by a timer having fired), one UNREF'd timeout armed for the oldest entry,\nand a drop from the socket `close` handler beside `releasePairingSecrets`.\n\nTHE INGRESS FILTER SKIPPED THE OBSERVATION. `tool.result.output` was scrubbed only when it was a\nSTRING, and every payload P1 made reachable is an object - `{stdout, stderr, exitCode}` and\n`{url, title, heading, accessibilitySnapshot}`. So the likeliest echo of all, a bash step's stdout,\nwas the one field the hosted filter did not look at, while the annotation to the left called this\nfilter the mirror of the daemon's egress redactor. It now walks `output` depth-bounded. BOTH\nredactors stay: the daemon's keeps the value off the network, this one still applies to a daemon\nthat is older, misconfigured or compromised. `screenshotB64` remains untouched - it is an image."
